.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt CORE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm core
.Nd memory image of a process a signal ended
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa core
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A process that
.Dv SIGQUIT , SIGILL , SIGTRAP , SIGABRT , SIGBUS , SIGFPE , SIGSEGV ,
.Dv SIGXCPU , SIGXFSZ
or
.Dv SIGSYS
ends by its default action leaves a core file, and so does one that ends
with
.Dv SIGSEGV
because the kernel could not build or read back a signal frame.
GDB reads the file with the program to show where the process was.
.Pp
The file is written only when the process is dumpable, as
.Xr prctl 2
describes, its soft
.Dv RLIMIT_CORE
is at least a page, 4096 bytes, and no other thread of it has begun to end
it.
The soft limit starts at 0, so a process asks for core files with
.Ic ulimit Fl c Cm unlimited
in the shell, or through
.Xr prlimit64 2 .
.Pp
The file is
.Pa core
in the working directory of the process.
Whatever has that name is unlinked first, and the file is created with
.Dv O_EXCL
and
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
mode 0600 less the umask, owned by the effective user and group IDs, so a
link planted at the name cannot send the file elsewhere.
A directory the process cannot write, or a descriptor table with no free
entry, leaves no file.
.Pp
The other threads of the process are sent
.Dv SIGKILL
before the file is written, and the process ends with the signal after
it.
Its wait status has the core bit,
.Dv WCOREDUMP
in
.Xr wait 2 ,
and
.Xr waitid 2
reports
.Dv CLD_DUMPED ,
only when the whole file was written.
.Pp
Linux names the file by
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/core_pattern
and may pipe it to a program; here the name is always
.Pa core .
Linux also writes
.Dv NT_SIGINFO ,
.Dv NT_FILE
and the TLS entries as notes, leaves out the pages of a private file
mapping the process never wrote, and writes a core file for a process
that is not dumpable when
.Pa /proc/sys/fs/suid_dumpable
asks; this kernel does none of these.
.Ss Format
The file is an ELF32 file of type
.Dv ET_CORE
for
.Dv EM_386 ,
laid out as Linux lays it out for i386.
Its first program header is a
.Dv PT_NOTE
segment with these notes, in this order:
.Bl -tag -width NT_PRXFPREG
.It Dv NT_PRSTATUS
The 144-byte
.Vt elf_prstatus
of the thread the signal ended: the signal, the low words of its pending
and blocked sets, its thread ID and the process's parent, group and
session, the CPU times, and its registers in the order of
.Vt user_regs_struct .
.It Dv NT_PRPSINFO
The 124-byte
.Vt elf_prpsinfo :
the nice value, the real user and group IDs, the process's IDs, the
program's name and the first 79 bytes of its arguments.
.It Dv NT_AUXV
The auxiliary vector the program started with.
.It Dv NT_FPREGSET
The thread's x87 state, as
.Vt user_i387_struct .
.It Dv NT_PRXFPREG
Named
.Dq LINUX ,
the thread's
.Ic fxsave
image.
.El
.Pp
.Dv NT_PRSTATUS ,
.Dv NT_FPREGSET
and
.Dv NT_PRXFPREG
follow for each other thread.
One
.Dv PT_LOAD
segment follows for each region of the address space, in address order,
with the region's protection.
A readable region has all its bytes in the file, from an offset aligned
to a page; a
.Dv PROT_NONE
region has none.
A page the process never touched is a hole in the file, and reads as
zeros.
.Pp
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_CORE
bounds the bytes written, holes excepted.
A file that reaches it stops there, and the wait status has no core bit.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width core
.It Pa core
The core file, in the working directory of the process that left it.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl ulimit -c unlimited
.Pp
.Pa tests/musl/coremusl.c
aborts children in a directory of
.Pa /dev/shm ,
where a link has the name, and reads the notes and the memory of the file
one leaves; a child whose limit is two pages leaves a file cut short, and
one whose limit is 0, or which is not dumpable, leaves none.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr prctl 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
